<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · artifact</p><h1 class="enc-title">ARA General Belgrano</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1938">1938</a>–<a href="/years/1982">1982</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">ARA General Belgrano was an Argentine light cruiser, built in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> as USS Phoenix, which survived the attack on Pearl Harbor and was sold to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/argentina">Argentina</a> in 1951. During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/falklands-war">Falklands War</a> it was torpedoed by the British submarine HMS Conqueror on 2 May 1982, with the loss of 323 lives, nearly half of Argentina's deaths in the conflict.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ara-general-belgrano.jpg" alt="ARA General Belgrano" width="320" height="226" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:ARA_General_Belgrano_underway.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The ship was built as USS Phoenix, the sixth ship of the Brooklyn-class cruiser design, by the New York Shipbuilding Corporation at Camden, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-jersey">New Jersey</a>. Work began in 1935 and she was launched in March 1938. She survived the Japanese attack on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/attack-on-pearl-harbor">Pearl Harbor</a> in 1941 undamaged and earned nine battle stars for service in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a>. She was placed in reserve at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philadelphia">Philadelphia</a> on 28 February 1946 and decommissioned on 3 July that year.</p><p>In October 1951 Phoenix was sold to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/argentina">Argentina</a> and renamed 17 de Octubre, after the Loyalty Day that was an important symbolic date for the party of President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/juan-peron">Juan Perón</a>. She was one of the main naval units that joined the 1955 coup that overthrew Perón, and was then renamed General Belgrano, after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuel-belgrano">Manuel Belgrano</a>, a lawyer who founded the School of Navigation in 1799 and fought for independence as a general from 1811 to 1819.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">How it was used</h2><p>After the Argentine invasion of the Falkland Islands, the United Kingdom declared a 200-nautical-mile Maritime Exclusion Zone on 12 April 1982, and it became a total exclusion zone on 30 April. General Belgrano left Ushuaia on 26 April and, with two destroyers and a tanker, formed Task Group 79.3. The British nuclear-powered submarine Conqueror detected her on 30 April.</p><p>On 1 May Admiral Juan Lombardo ordered Argentine naval units to launch a massive attack the next day. British intelligence intercepted the signal, and on 2 May Prime Minister <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/margaret-thatcher">Margaret Thatcher</a> and her War Cabinet agreed to allow an attack outside the exclusion zone. At 14:57 Conqueror fired three torpedoes, two of which hit the cruiser. One blew off her bow and the other exploded in the aft machine room, damaging her electrical power and preventing a radio distress call. Captain Bonzo ordered the crew to abandon ship at 16:24. Argentine and Chilean ships rescued 772 men from 3 to 5 May, and 323 were killed, 321 crew members and two civilians.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>After the sinking the Argentine fleet returned to base and took little part in the rest of the war. The attack came 14 hours after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a>'s President Fernando Belaúnde proposed a peace plan. Its legality was disputed. The Argentine government called it a treacherous act of armed aggression, while Captain Bonzo said in 2003 that it was an act of war and that the submarine had followed the accepted rules, and a 1994 Argentine Defence Ministry report called it a legal act of war. The wreck was not found in a 2003 search, and the area is classed as a war grave under Argentine law.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “ARA General Belgrano” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
